From e0932c35b29208c2bf81f6001e339423655fff86 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mate Soos <soos.mate@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 23:10:22 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Fix repeated variables in a lineral being OR-ed instead of
 XOR-ed

Bug: the XNF parser builds a lineral such as "1+1+2" by setting the bit of
each variable it reads, so a repeated variable is kept instead of cancelled:
"1+1+2" was read as x1+x2 rather than x2. That silently changes the formula
and gives wrong answers. For example

  p xnf 2 2
  1+1+2 0
  -2 0

is UNSAT (the first clause is x2, the second is not-x2), but xorcle answered
"s SATISFIABLE" with "v 1 -2 0", an assignment that falsifies the first
clause. Xorricane and CryptoMiniSat both answer UNSAT.

Fix: flip the variable's bit instead of setting it, so x + x = 0.

Found with hand-written edge cases next to differential fuzzing
(xnf_fuzzer.py), whose generator never repeats a variable in a lineral.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/io/DimacsParser.cpp | 3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/io/DimacsParser.cpp b/src/io/DimacsParser.cpp
index 2d92e14..401b36c 100644
--- a/src/io/DimacsParser.cpp
+++ b/src/io/DimacsParser.cpp
@@ -113,7 +113,8 @@ algebra::Equation parse_clause_token(const std::string& token, std::size_t num_v
             throw std::runtime_error("Variable out of range: " + variable_token);
         }
 
-        equation.set(static_cast<std::size_t>(variable - 1));
+        // flip, not set: a repeated variable cancels (x + x = 0)
+        equation.flip(static_cast<std::size_t>(variable - 1));
 
         if (plus_pos == std::string::npos) {
             break;
